gm-method: a frozen/idle-looking animation with a state machine reporting a normal logical state (no error, valid state name) and a running mixer.update() call every frame is not proof the animation pipeline works -- sample a bone's actual world/local rotation across N consecutive frames directly. Two real, independent bugs can each look like "nothing is wrong upstream": (1) a bone-name remap gap between a shared animation-clip rig and a per-character skeleton silently drops every track whose name doesn't match, leaving only coincidental root/hip matches moving; (2) a downstream system that runs AFTER the animation mixer in the same frame (e.g. a humanoid-retargeting update() call) can silently overwrite what the mixer just wrote, so the mixer looks like it's running correctly right up until the moment its output is stomped one line later in the frame loop. Check both write order and actual per-bone output, not just the state-machine's self-report.